900-82be-dab9e519a2ec" data-active="false" data-pf-type="Accordion3.Header"><svg class="pf-accordion-icon pfa-chevron-right pfa-arrow" style="width:auto;fill:currentcolor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><path d="M278.6 233.4c12.5 12.5 12.5 32.8 0 45.3l-160 160c-12.5 12.5-32.8 12.5-45.3 0s-12.5-32.8 0-45.3L210.7 256 73.4 118.6c-12.5-12.5-12.5-32.8 0-45.3s32.8-12.5 45.3 0l160 160z"></path></svg><span>Understanding The Science of Pheromones?</span></div></summary><div class="sc-fYEDqr bVoGwk pf-411_ pf-accordion-body" data-pf-expandable="true" data-pf-type="Accordion3.Content"><div class="pf-accordion-display-content"><div data-pf-type="Accordion3.Flex.Content" class="sc-gvXgmx lipwAq pf-412_"><div class="sc-dhKdPU hjOBND pf-413_ pf-r pf-c-cm pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:0px" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:12;--c-md:12;--c-lg:12"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-iapVNj iUcVvL pf-414_"><p class="sc-gFVuLS jpmtwg pf-415_ pf-text-1" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Pheromones are chemical messengers released by animals, including humans, that trigger social responses in others of the same species. They influence behaviors like mating, territory marking, and social bonding. In humans, pheromones affect attraction and social interactions, used in perfumes and colognes to enhance appeal.</p></div></div></div><div class="sc-dhKdPU hjOBND pf-417_ pf-r pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:15px" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:12;--c-md:12;--c-lg:12"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-iapVNj iUcVvL pf-418_"></div></div></div></div></div></div></details><details class="sc-bvrmaJ hVxpDQ pf-419_" data-pf-type="Accordion3.Content.Wrapper"><summary><div class="sc-bCMEPS brrCCO pf-420_ pf-bg-lazy pf-anchor pf-header-item-wrapper" data-header-id="5da3ff5a-a8bb-477a-9093-c064e1818c19" data-active="false" data-pf-type="Accordion3.Header"><svg class="pf-accordion-icon pfa-chevron-right pfa-arrow" style="width:auto;fill:currentcolor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><path d="M278.6 233.4c12.5 12.5 12.5 32.8 0 45.3l-160 160c-12.5 12.5-32.8 12.5-45.3 0s-12.5-32.8 0-45.3L210.7 256 73.4 118.6c-12.5-12.5-12.5-32.8 0-45.3s32.8-12.5 45.3 0l160 160z"></path></svg><span>How Pheromones are made?</span></div></summary><div class="sc-fYEDqr bVoGwk pf-422_ pf-accordion-body" data-pf-expandable="true" data-pf-type="Accordion3.Content"><div class="pf-accordion-display-content"><div data-pf-type="Accordion3.Flex.Content" class="sc-gvXgmx lipwAq pf-423_"><div class="sc-dhKdPU hjOBND pf-424_ pf-r pf-c-cm pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:0px" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:12;--c-md:12;--c-lg:12"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-iapVNj iUcVvL pf-425_"><p class="sc-gFVuLS jpmtwg pf-426_ pf-text-1" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Pheromones are made in specialized glands in the body, such as skin, sweat glands, and reproductive organs. They are synthesized, then secreted onto the skin or into bodily fluids. Once released, they disperse into the environment and are detected by others through their olfactory system, influencing behaviors and attraction.</p></div></div></div><div class="sc-dhKdPU hjOBND pf-428_ pf-r pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:15px" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:12;--c-md:12;--c-lg:12"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-iapVNj iUcVvL pf-429_"></div></div></div></div></div></div></details><details class="sc-bvrmaJ hVxpDQ pf-430_" data-pf-type="Accordion3.Content.Wrapper"><summary><div class="sc-bCMEPS brrCCO pf-431_ pf-bg-lazy pf-anchor pf-header-item-wrapper" data-header-id="af2a5b7a-7ef0-4127-bee9-dad125343b2f" data-active="false" data-pf-type="Accordion3.Header"><svg class="pf-accordion-icon pfa-chevron-right pfa-arrow" style="width:auto;fill:currentcolor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 512"><path d="M278.6 233.4c12.5 12.5 12.5 32.8 0 45.3l-160 160c-12.5 12.5-32.8 12.5-45.3 0s-12.5-32.8 0-45.3L210.7 256